# Feedbacks's settings. For `captify dev`, copy this file to .env.local, which is read before .env
# and never committed. captify dev, and in production the estate, set the CAPTIFY_* settings the
# SDK reads (Titan, Platform and the workload key), so there are none of those here.

# --- captify dev: where the private estate keeps its data -----------------------------------------
# Leave the AWS settings unset and run `captify dev --memory` to keep the rest in memory instead.

# Platform's records, and this app's own tables in every tenant's schema of the apps database
# beside them, in a Postgres you are a superuser of. With --memory, the app's tables still need
# it, in a database made for the run. Add CAPTIFY_DATABASE_PASSWORD if the URL has none.
# CAPTIFY_DATABASE_URL=postgres://postgres:dev@127.0.0.1:5432/captify_dev
# Sessions and files go in DynamoDB and S3: an emulator of both, such as awsim or LocalStack, or
# with no endpoint, the development account your credentials name. DynamoDB Local has no S3, so
# it works only beside an S3 emulator: in place of AWS_ENDPOINT_URL, name DynamoDB Local with
# AWS_ENDPOINT_URL_DYNAMODB and the S3 emulator with AWS_ENDPOINT_URL_S3.
# AWS_ENDPOINT_URL=http://127.0.0.1:4566
# AWS_REGION=us-east-1
# AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID=test
# AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=test

# --- captify dev: real sign-in, in place of the mock directories ----------------------------------
# A Cognito user pool and its client. The estate is then at http://localhost:<port>, which the
# client must allow as a callback (/_titan/callback). Test people are found by email, and each
# needs the custom:tenant_id attribute set to their tenant in captify.dev.json, readable by the
# client, or Titan refuses them. People are looked up in the pool with your AWS credentials, at
# the issuer's host when it is not AWS's own (as awsim's https://<host>/cognito/<pool>), and
# otherwise at Cognito itself even when AWS_ENDPOINT_URL names an emulator, so those must then be
# real. AWS_ENDPOINT_URL_COGNITO_IDENTITY_PROVIDER names another endpoint for either. Platform
# reads and manages the pool's users and groups with them too; the pool keeps its own groups,
# approval and sign-in, so those of captify.dev.json apply to the mock directory alone.
# CAPTIFY_IDP_ISSUER=https://cognito-idp.us-east-1.amazonaws.com/us-east-1_EXAMPLE
# CAPTIFY_IDP_CLIENT_ID=
# CAPTIFY_IDP_CLIENT_SECRET=
# The same for staff, with your email as "operator" in captify.dev.json.
# CAPTIFY_STAFF_IDP_ISSUER=
# CAPTIFY_STAFF_IDP_CLIENT_ID=
# CAPTIFY_STAFF_IDP_CLIENT_SECRET=

